<DocsPage
  title="Permissions & Safety"
  description="Agents run real commands on your machine. Here is what they can reach by default, how to tighten it, and how to think about the trade-off."
  currentPath="/docs/permissions/"
  next={next}
>
  <h2 id="the-model">The Model</h2>
  <p>
    Agents in Molt Code run locally as real subprocesses, in your project's
    directory, with your files. That is the source of both their usefulness and
    the care they deserve.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two settings govern a run, and both live on the <strong>agent</strong> rather
    than the thread:
  </p>
  <ul class="fields">
    <li>
      <strong>Permission mode</strong>
      <span>Whether the agent asks before acting, or proceeds on its own.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sandbox mode</strong>
      <span>How confined the agent's commands are — what it can read, write, and
      reach beyond the project.</span>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Because they sit on the agent, they travel with every tag automatically. A
    reviewer configured read-only stays read-only no matter which thread you tag
    it in, and you never have to remember to re-apply it.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>The default is permissive.</strong> A tagged run approves its own
      tool calls and has full access to your machine unless you configure the
      agent otherwise. There is no confirmation dialog between typing an
      <code>@handle</code> and work starting — the tag is the approval.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is deliberate: it is what makes the post-and-tag loop fast. But it
      means the agent you tag should be one you configured on purpose.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="approval-prompts">Approval Prompts</h2>
  <p>
    Set an agent to a stricter permission mode and it will pause before
    consequential actions, asking you to approve or deny. The run resumes from
    exactly where it stopped.
  </p>
  <p>
    The trade-off is real in both directions. Approval prompts on a long
    refactor mean an agent sitting idle waiting for you. No prompts on an agent
    with a vague brief means finding out afterwards. A reasonable split:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Permissive</strong> for agents doing well-understood work in a repository under version control, where <code>git diff</code> is your safety net.</li>
    <li><strong>Restricted</strong> for anything touching infrastructure, deployments, or systems where a mistake is not one <code>git checkout</code> away.</li>
    <li><strong>Read-only</strong> for reviewers and investigators. An agent that only reads cannot break anything, and reviewing is most of what you want from it anyway.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Version control is the practical backstop. Working in a Git repository with
    a clean tree before you tag turns most agent mistakes into a discarded diff.
  </p>

  <h2 id="secrets">Secrets</h2>
  <p>
    Projects can hold secrets — API keys, database URLs, deploy tokens — that
    processes and agents reference by name rather than by value. The value stays
    managed rather than pasted into a file, a prompt, or a thread.
  </p>
  <p>
    Reference secrets by name in project configuration. Never paste a live
    credential into a post: threads are durable and shared, which is exactly what
    you do not want from a secret.
  </p>
  <p>
    Note that CLI authentication is separate — each engine holds its own login.
    See <a href="/docs/connect-clis/">Connect CLIs &amp; Models</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-agents-can-reach">What Agents Can Reach</h2>
  <p>
    A tagged agent can reach, subject to its permission and sandbox settings:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The project directory</strong> it is running in — reading and, unless restricted, writing.</li>
    <li><strong>The thread</strong> that tagged it, for context, and the ability to reply to it.</li>
    <li><strong>Project surfaces</strong> — issues, knowledge pages, designs, processes.</li>
    <li><strong>Its attached MCP servers</strong>, which are configured per agent. See <a href="/docs/mcp/">MCP Servers</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Some boundaries hold regardless of settings. File artifacts must be
    project-relative — an agent cannot attach a path that escapes the repository
    — and it cannot attach another project's issues to your thread. Those are
    enforced rather than merely discouraged.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything an agent did is inspectable afterwards: open the session from its
    reply and read the run in full. If you are ever unsure what happened, that
    trace is the answer, and it is one click from the thread.
  </p>
</DocsPage>
